GITHUB – Basic Operations Using the Browser
1. Creating a New Folder

GitHub does not allow you to create empty folders. To create a new folder:

Open the repository (e.g., first-test-repo).
Click Add file → Create new file.
In the filename field, enter the complete path:

test/placeholder.txt

Enter any text in the file editor.
Click Commit changes.
The test folder will be created automatically, containing placeholder.txt.
2. Uploading a File from Your Computer into a Folder
Open the repository.
Navigate to the destination folder (e.g., test).
Click Add file → Upload files.
Select the file from your computer or drag and drop it into the upload area.
Enter a commit message (e.g., Upload test.txt).
Click Commit changes.

The file will be uploaded directly into the selected folder.

3. Moving a File by Changing Its Path

You can move an existing file by editing its path.

Example: moving text2.txt from the repository's root directory into the test folder.

Open text2.txt in the repository's root directory.
Click the pencil icon (Edit this file).
In the filename field, change the path:

From: text2.txt

To: test/text2.txt

Click Commit changes.
Confirm the commit.

GitHub records the path change, and the file will appear inside the test folder.

4. Important Concept: Commits

A commit records a change made to the repository.

A commit is required whenever you:

Create a file.
Upload a file.
Edit a file.
Move a file by changing its path.

Tip: Always use short but descriptive commit messages, for example:

Create test folder
Upload test.txt
Move text2.txt to test folder
Update README

A good commit message makes it easier to track the history of your repository.

Note: When you change a file's path, GitHub records a repository change. Git may recognize it as a rename or move, but technically it is not a separate cut-and-paste operation.
